"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[5932],{5932:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"base-12","slug":"base-12","trackId":"base","order":13,"title":"Forecast Sellers","subtitle":"Clear Rules for Protecting Capital","estimatedMinutes":14,"access":"free","quizCount":3,"blueprintCount":0,"trainerIds":["HUB-CALC-05","HUB-CALC-06"],"markdown":"Lesson 12 \xb7 9 min\\n\\n## Forecast Sellers\\n\\nHow the people selling you forecasts make money\\n\\nIn the lesson on exchanges, we explored why trading is a profession rather than easy money. In the previous lesson, we gathered the most common mistakes that cause beginners to lose money. Yet one question remains that many people never ask themselves. If earning consistently from trading is so difficult, where do the people selling forecasts, signals and courses get their expensive cars, trips and screenshots showing 500% profits?\\n\\nThe answer is unpleasant but simple:\\n\\nmost of them do not earn their money in the market. They earn it from you.\\n\\nThis does not mean everyone writing about cryptocurrency is a scammer. But to assess any advice, you first need to understand the finances of the person giving it.\\n\\n### The Main Question: Who Pays the Blogger?\\n\\nEvery media business has a business model. A television channel has advertising. A newspaper has subscriptions. A bank analyst receives a salary from a bank. Crypto influencers are no different. Before listening to a forecast, ask:\\n\\nhow does this person make money, and does their income depend on whether I make money?\\n\\nVery often, the answer is that their income depends on how actively you act, rather than on whether you profit. Let us examine the main sources.\\n\\n### 1. Exchange Referral Links\\n\\nThis is the most common and least noticeable source of income. Almost all major crypto exchanges pay partners a share of the fees generated by referred users. For example, the Bybit affiliate program promises partners up to 50% of referred clients\' trading fees. Other major exchanges offer similar terms. The mechanism works like this:\\n- a blogger provides a link “with a bonus for followers”;\\n- you register through it;\\n- each time you open and close a trade, the exchange charges a fee;\\n- the blogger receives a share of that fee. For life.\\n\\nOne important detail: the blogger receives a share of the fee, not your profit. You profit, and the blogger earns. You lose, and the blogger still earns.\\n\\nTap to zoom in\\n\\n#### A Simple Calculation\\n\\nSuppose you have $1 000. You trade futures with 20x leverage. Each position is therefore worth $20 000. An illustrative futures fee at a major exchange is around 0,05% on entry and the same on exit. This gives:\\n\\n$20 000 \xd7 0,05% \xd7 2 = $20 per trade.\\n\\nThat is 2% of your deposit. For one trade. Even if the price does not move. Now imagine you follow the channel\'s advice and make 5 trades a day.\\n\\n$20 \xd7 5 = $100 per day. That is 10% of your deposit.\\n\\nIf the trades themselves break even, fees consume all your money in 10 trading days. Meanwhile, a blogger with a 40% share receives $40 a day from you. With 100 followers like you, that is $4 000 a day. Without placing a single trade themselves.\\n\\nIt therefore benefits the blogger if you trade more often, with higher leverage and larger amounts. These are precisely the things it is often in your interest to avoid.\\n\\nThat is why these channels contain so many calls to “not miss the entry,” “enter with leverage” and “add to the position.”\\n\\nYou have $1 000 and a blogger\'s link. Who earns? Leverage 20x \xb7 Trades per day 5.\\n\\n$20\\n\\nper trade\\n\\n$100\\n\\nper day\\n\\n$40\\n\\nto the blogger per day. Even if the trades break even, fees consume the deposit in 10 days. A blogger with a 40% share gets $40 a day from you alone — or $4 000 from one hundred followers.\\n\\n### 2. Paid Signals and VIP Channels\\n\\nAnother popular model is a free channel alongside a private paid “VIP” channel with signals. You are shown a few winning trades, then offered a subscription for $50, $100 or $300 a month. Ask yourself a simple question.\\n\\nIf someone can consistently earn 10% a month, why sell signals for $100?\\n\\nLet us calculate. At 10% monthly with compounding, $10 000 becomes roughly $31 000 after a year. After 5 years, roughly $3 000 000. A person with that skill does not need followers. They need only their own capital and time.\\n\\nSelling signals therefore almost always means one of two things:\\n- the strategy is not as consistent as advertised;\\n- the main income comes from subscriptions and referral fees rather than trading.\\n\\nSignals also often come with a referral link. The blogger earns twice: from your subscription and from the fees on trades you place using their signals.\\n\\n### 3. Courses, Mentorship and “Lifestyle”\\n\\nThe third source is trading education. Education itself is not bad. This course is education too. The problem arises when a dream is sold in place of knowledge.\\n\\nAn expensive car in the shot, a hotel, a sea view, a profit screenshot. None proves that the person earns money in the market. A car can be rented for a day. A screenshot can come from a demo account. Course sales may well have paid for the hotel.\\n\\nA course seller\'s lifestyle proves only one thing: the course sells well.\\n\\n### 4. Paid Token Promotion\\n\\nThe fourth source is projects paying bloggers to mention their tokens. Such advertising is often not disclosed and looks like a personal opinion: “I found an interesting project,” or “my favorite for this cycle.”\\n\\nThe best-known example is the US Securities and Exchange Commission\'s (SEC) 2022 penalty against Kim Kardashian. She was paid $250 000 for an Instagram post about the EMAX token without disclosing to followers that it was an advertisement. The result was $1,26 million in penalties and payments.\\n\\nCelebrities are only the tip of the iceberg.\\n\\nIn September 2025, independent blockchain investigator ZachXBT published a leaked spreadsheet for a token\'s promotional campaign. According to his findings, more than 160 crypto bloggers agreed to payments ranging from $50 to $60 000 per post. Fewer than 5 disclosed the advertising.\\n\\nYour favorite blogger\'s “independent opinion” may therefore be a paid post.\\n\\n### 5. Pump and Dump: Profiting Directly From Followers\\n\\nThis is the harshest scheme.\\n- A blogger or associated people buy an obscure token in advance.\\n- The blogger publishes an enthusiastic post: “This is the next x100.”\\n- Followers buy and the price rises.\\n- Those who bought first sell to followers at the high price.\\n- The price falls. Followers are left with losses.\\n\\nThe blogger does not even have to lie about buying the token. They really did buy it. Just earlier and cheaper than you. In 2025, Argentina\'s president Javier Milei posted about the LIBRA memecoin. Its price surged, then collapsed a few hours later after the largest early holders sold their tokens. A judicial investigation into Milei was opened.\\n\\nCrypto market analysis company Solidus Labs calculated that 98,6% of memecoins launched on the popular Pump.fun platform showed signs of pump-and-dump schemes.\\n\\nWhen urged to buy an obscure token urgently, remember that you may be providing liquidity for someone else\'s exit rather than investing.\\n\\n### 6. Copy Trading and “Managed Accounts”\\n\\nAnother offer is “copy my trades” or “give me your money to manage.” Many exchanges have a copy-trading feature. You connect to a trader and their trades are automatically replicated in your account. The trader receives a share of followers\' profits. Notice the asymmetry. When a trade wins, the trader receives their share.\\n\\nWhen a trade loses, the entire loss is yours. The trader risks almost nothing while receiving part of the upside. This creates an incentive to take more risk than you would yourself. As for “money management” by an unlicensed person on the internet, it offers no protection at best and is an ordinary financial pyramid at worst.\\n\\n### 7. Attention as a Product\\n\\nA subtler mechanism also exists. Social media algorithms promote content that triggers emotions. A confident prediction that “Bitcoin will reach $200 000” gets more views than an honest “Nobody knows where the price will go.” Almost nobody checks forecasts six months later. Wrong predictions are forgotten; a successful one becomes a legend and permanent proof of expertise.\\n\\nA study by researchers from Berkeley and Lausanne illustrates this clearly. They analyzed financial influencers\' advice on StockTwits and found:\\n- only 28% gave advice that was profitable on average;\\n- 16% showed no discernible skill;\\n- 56% gave advice that caused followers to lose money on average.\\n\\nThe most troubling point: those whose advice led to losses had more followers than those who demonstrated actual skill. Popularity and expertise are different things. Sometimes they are opposites.\\n\\n### Why Their Screenshots Always Look Good\\n\\nEven a person without skill can make their statistics look perfect. Here are some methods.\\n\\n#### Survivorship Bias\\n\\nImagine 1 024 people randomly predicting whether the price will rise or fall. After the first round, roughly half are right: 512 people. After the second, 256. After the third, 128. After the fourth, 64. After the fifth round, 32 people have been right 5 times in a row.\\n\\nNone has any skill. It is simply mathematics. Yet those 32 people look like geniuses from the outside. They are the people who appear in your feed. The other 992 quietly deleted their channels.\\n\\n1 024 people predict exchange rates. At random.\\n\\n1024\\n\\nnobody has made a correct prediction yet\\n\\n#### Two Channels\\n\\nA blogger runs two channels. One says “buy,” the other says “sell.” The losing channel is then closed, while the winning channel is presented as proof of accuracy.\\n\\n#### Deleted Signals\\n\\nFailed forecasts are simply deleted or edited. Only successful ones remain.\\n\\n#### Screenshots Without Context\\n\\nA +500% profit looks impressive. But the screenshot does not show that the position was $20 with 100x leverage, that another account was liquidated, or that this was a demo account.\\n\\nA profit screenshot proves nothing. Only a complete history of all trades, published before the result was known, provides evidence.\\n\\n### What Governments Are Doing About It\\n\\nRegulators in many countries have already turned their attention to financial influencers.\\n\\nThe source states that Russia has prohibited cryptocurrency advertising since August 2024, and that from September 1, 2026, only legal intermediaries may advertise their services, accompanied by risk information. A blogger\'s link to a foreign exchange “with a bonus” should therefore be treated as advertising even if it is not labeled. These are dated regulatory claims; verify current applicable law before relying on them.\\n\\nIn February 2026, the Bank of Russia published a report on regulating financial influencers. According to a NAFI study cited by the regulator, 37% of investors learn from specialist Telegram channels, and 29% rely on bloggers\' advice.\\n\\nThe central bank proposed a register of financial influencers, qualification requirements and mandatory conflict-of-interest disclosure. Following discussion, the regulator plans to begin with a voluntary “whitelist” and code of ethics. While the rules are still developing, you must check the source yourself.\\n\\n### Checklist: How to Assess Someone Selling You Forecasts\\n\\nBefore following someone\'s advice, answer seven questions. 1. How do they earn money? Referral links, subscriptions, courses, advertising or managing other people\'s money. 2. Do they earn when I lose? If so, your interests differ. 3. Is there a complete track record? Every signal, including losing ones, with a publication date preceding the event.\\n\\n4. Do they discuss risks and losses, or only profit? 5. Do they rush you? “Last chance,” “enter right now,” “10 minutes left.” 6. Do they disclose advertising and whether they hold the token they discuss?\\n\\n7. If the strategy works, why are they selling it?\\n\\nIf most answers are concerning, it is better to pass.\\n\\n### This Applies to Us Too\\n\\nLet us be honest: we are a media business too, and we also earn money. From this course, subscriptions and advertising. Ask us these questions too. That is normal. A good author will not be offended by “How do you make money?” They will simply answer.\\n\\n### The Main Point\\n\\nMost people selling trading forecasts, signals and courses earn their money from an audience rather than the market. Their income rises when you trade more often, take more risk and buy what they recommend. Even when you lose money doing it. Popularity does not equal expertise. Screenshots do not equal statistics. A confident tone does not equal knowledge.\\n\\nBefore asking “Where will the price go?”, ask “Who will earn money if I follow this advice?”\\n\\nThis concludes the course. You now have a foundation: you understand how cryptocurrencies work, how to store, transfer and buy them, and where the risks begin. Above all, you know which questions to ask before trusting anyone with your money.\\n\\n### Check Yourself 1 / 3\\n\\nA blogger provides an exchange referral link and recommends trading leveraged futures every day. How do they earn money?\\n\\n### Terms Used in This Lesson:\\n- **Bitcoin** — The first cryptocurrency, designed as money without intermediaries. Its supply is capped at 21 million coins. It has existed for 15+ years, survived several crashes and recovered each time. “Digital gold.”\\n- **Blockchain** — A record of all transactions that everyone can inspect: entries can be added, but old entries cannot be changed. It is stored simultaneously on millions of computers, making falsification of the history physically impossible\\n- **Liquidation** — The forced sale of collateral after its value falls. In crypto, code simply executes the condition: nobody calls or warns you first\\n- **Liquidity** — The ability to exchange an asset for cash quickly without losses. An emergency fund must be liquid, available whenever needed\\n- **Memecoin** — A cryptocurrency without technology or a substantive idea (DOGE, SHIB), sustained by hype. It can rise hundreds of times, but more often collapses to zero. A lottery ticket, not an investment\\n- **Compound Interest** — Interest earned on interest. A long-term investor’s strongest ally: 10 000 ₽ today can be worth more in 20 years than 100 000 ₽ invested in 10 years\\n- **SEC (Securities and Exchange Commission)** — The American regulator created after the 1929 crisis. In 2024, it approved spot Bitcoin ETFs, opening crypto to institutional investors\\n- **Futures Contract** — An agreement to buy or sell something in the future at a price agreed today. Futures prices show market expectations, from oil prices to Federal Reserve interest-rate decisions. These are expectations, not forecasts; the market is regularly wrong\\n- **Token** — A digital asset that exists within a network, such as USDT or USDC. The same token can exist on different networks, and those are different versions of it\\n- **Leverage** — A position larger than your own funds, financed with borrowing: at 10x leverage, $1 000 opens a $10 000 position. Price movements affect your capital 10 times more strongly, in either direction\\n- **Copy Trading** — Your account automatically copies another trader’s trades. The trader shares in profits; losses remain entirely yours\\n- **Pump and Dump** — A scheme involving buying an obscure token first, promoting it through posts and selling to followers at the peak. The buyers are left with losses\\n\\n### End-of-Lesson Quiz (“Check Yourself”):\\n**Question 1: A blogger provides an exchange referral link and recommends trading leveraged futures every day. How do they earn money?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: They receive a share only of your profit, so they have an interest in your success\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: They receive a share of trading fees, so they earn from all your trades, including losing ones\\n- ⚪ Option 3: They receive nothing; the link only provides a bonus for followers\\n\\n**Question 2: A channel shows 5 successful forecasts in a row. What does that tell you?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: The author is definitely a professional and can be trusted with money\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: Very little by itself: what matters is the complete history of every forecast, published before the event\\n- ⚪ Option 3: The sixth forecast is also certain to come true\\n\\n**Question 3: A famous blogger urges you to buy a new obscure token immediately before it ‘takes off.’ What is the safest action?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: Buy with all your money before the price rises\\n- ⚪ Option 2: Buy with leverage to earn more\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 3: Take your time and investigate who could have bought the token earlier, whether the blogger was paid and where growth would come from\\n\\n\\n---","blueprints":[],"quiz":[{"id":"q-base-12-1","prompt":"A blogger provides an exchange referral link and recommends trading leveraged futures every day. How do they earn money?","options":[{"id":"base-12-1-1","label":"They receive a share only of your profit, so they have an interest in your success","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. They receive a share of trading fees, so they earn from all your trades, including losing ones"},{"id":"base-12-1-2","label":"They receive a share of trading fees, so they earn from all your trades, including losing ones","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. They receive a share of trading fees, so they earn from all your trades, including losing ones"},{"id":"base-12-1-3","label":"They receive nothing; the link only provides a bonus for followers","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. They receive a share of trading fees, so they earn from all your trades, including losing ones"}],"explanation":"They receive a share of trading fees, so they earn from all your trades, including losing ones","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:2700–2703 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-12-2","prompt":"A channel shows 5 successful forecasts in a row. What does that tell you?","options":[{"id":"base-12-2-1","label":"The author is definitely a professional and can be trusted with money","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Very little by itself: what matters is the complete history of every forecast, published before the event"},{"id":"base-12-2-2","label":"Very little by itself: what matters is the complete history of every forecast, published before the event","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Very little by itself: what matters is the complete history of every forecast, published before the event"},{"id":"base-12-2-3","label":"The sixth forecast is also certain to come true","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Very little by itself: what matters is the complete history of every forecast, published before the event"}],"explanation":"Very little by itself: what matters is the complete history of every forecast, published before the event","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:2705–2708 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-12-3","prompt":"A famous blogger urges you to buy a new obscure token immediately before it ‘takes off.’ What is the safest action?","options":[{"id":"base-12-3-1","label":"Buy with all your money before the price rises","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Take your time and investigate who could have bought the token earlier, whether the blogger was paid and where growth would come from"},{"id":"base-12-3-2","label":"Buy with leverage to earn more","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Take your time and investigate who could have bought the token earlier, whether the blogger was paid and where growth would come from"},{"id":"base-12-3-3","label":"Take your time and investigate who could have bought the token earlier, whether the blogger was paid and where growth would come from","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Take your time and investigate who could have bought the token earlier, whether the blogger was paid and where growth would come from"}],"explanation":"Take your time and investigate who could have bought the token earlier, whether the blogger was paid and where growth would come from","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:2710–2713 (Verbatim)"}],"source":{"file":"00_FREE_GIFT_MakeCash_Crypto_And_Capital_Base_15_Lessons.md","sha256":"88a3efb162434bb8a9b3b000caee579d9c27005a8ea46ddf4ef9cafb50dc45cc","lineStart":2482,"lineEnd":2718}}')}}]);